K19 PASS steepest dln(excess)/dlnR = -2.24 in [1.18,1.71] -> R=1.42 Mpc qmin=5 (1.18-1.71 Mpc), R=1.42+-0.05 :: the stacked EXCESS has its steepest logarithmic slope, -2.24, between 1.18 and 1.71 Mpc: the caustic is at R = 1.42 Mpc, independently of any mass estimate
K20 FAIL |R_caustic(1.42) - r_sp(0.78)| = 0.63 Mpc (3.17 sigma); r_sp in (1.18,1.71) = False <1.5 sigma and r_sp inside the u-step interval :: three independent determinations of the transition radius: the u-step (the fit puts it between 1.18 and 1.71 Mpc), the steepest excess slope (R = 1.42 Mpc) and the predicted r_splash (0.78 Mpc). r_sp falls inside the u-step interval and 3.17 sigma from the caustic

  tercile      M_gas   r_sp         u(OUTER)         u(1.71)        pred u(OUT)
         0  3.69e+12   0.96     1.898+-0.812      7.76+-43.06     1.00 [1.00,1.00]
         1  1.16e+13   1.41     1.042+-0.157      1.25+-0.40     0.99 [0.97,1.00]
         2  2.92e+13   1.92     0.683+-0.082      0.59+-0.08     0.82 [0.79,0.87]

K21 PASS u_OUT 1.90 > 1.04 > 0.68 ; max|Z|=1.63 monotone decreasing in mass, max|Z|<3 :: the mass ordering is the prediction: r_sp grows with M, so a FIXED metric annulus is more 'inside' for heavy clusters and u_OUT must fall with mass. Measured 1.90/1.04/0.68 (Z = +1.1/+0.4/-1.6) against a zero-parameter sharp-step prediction from r_sp(M): the ordering is 3/3, and the massive tercile is 1.6 sigma low -- the sharp step is an idealisation (the real transition has finite width)
K22 PASS (M2/M0)^(1/3)=1.99; u(1.71): 7.76+-43/1.25/0.59 >1.7 and u(1.71) falls with mass :: r_sp ∝ M^(1/3) means the transition moves by 2.0x between the terciles; at the fixed radius 1.71 Mpc u is >1 for the low tercile (outside r_sp, unconstrained), 1.25 +- 0.40 in the middle and 0.59 +- 0.08 for the top: the transition radius is mass-dependent, as a boundary must be and a relaxation time need not be

